TITLE: A Lightweight Language-Model-Driven Agentic Framework for Intrusion Prediction, Detection, and Mitigation in 6G-Enabled IoT Networks
TEXT: Received 23 May 2026; revised 26 June 2026; accepted 14 August 2026. Date of publication 20 August 2026; date of current version 27 August 2026. Digital Object Identiﬁer 10.1109/OJCOMS.2026.3725365 A Lightweight Language-Model-Driven Agentic Framework for Intrusion Prediction, Detection, and Mitigation in 6G-Enabled IoT Networks ALAEDDINE DIAF 1, ABDELAZIZ AMARA KORBA 1,2 (Senior Member, IEEE), WAEL JAAFAR 3 (Senior Member, IEEE), NOUR ELISLEM KARABADJI 4, AND YACINE GHAMRI-DOUDANE 5 (Senior Member, IEEE) 1Laboratoire Re´ seaux et Syste´ mes (LRS), Badji Mokhtar - Annaba University, Annaba 23000, Algeria 2Computer Science Department, German University of Technology in Oman, Halban Campus, Muscat 130, Oman 3E´ cole de Technologie Supe´ rieure (E´ TS), University of Quebec, Montreal, QC H3C 1K3, Canada 4National Higher School of Technology and Engineering, Laboratoire De Technologies Des Syste` mes E´ nerge´ tiques (LTSE) E3360100, Annaba 23005, Algeria 5L3i, La Rochelle University, 17042 La Rochelle, France CORRESPONDING AUTHOR: W. JAAFAR (wael.jaafar@etsmtl.ca) This work was supported in part by the Mitacs Globalink Research Award (GRA) Program and in part by the Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada (NSERC) Discovery under Grant CRSNG RGPIN-2023-03757. ABSTRACT The extensive deployment of Internet of Things (IoT) devices in emerging 6G-enabled environments, characterized by massive connectivity, distributed edge intelligence, and ultra-low-latency communication, allows cyber threats to evade detection until malicious activities manifest. Conventional intrusion detection systems remain fundamentally reactive, identifying threats only after suspicious patterns become observable in network traﬃc, which hinders both proactive threat anticipation and timely mitigation. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a role-based multi-agent cybersecurity framework for 6G-enabled IoT networks that enables proactive intrusion prediction, real-time detection, and knowledge-driven threat mitigation. The framework establishes a coordinated defense loop composed of three specialized agents: (i) a prediction agent that leverages Small Language Models (SLMs) to model packet-level temporal dependencies and proactively forecast malicious traﬃc sequences, (ii) a detection agent that performs real-time traﬃc classiﬁcation using a lightweight gradient boosting model suitable for low-latency edge environments, and (iii) a knowledge graph-driven mitigation agent that maps detected threats to corresponding defensive countermeasures. Extensive evaluation on the CICIoT2023 dataset demonstrates the eﬀectiveness of the proposed framework, achieving 98.24% accuracy in proactive packet-level detection using forecasted packets and 99.94% accuracy in real-time ﬂow-based detection. The proposed framework combines structured multi-agent coordination, low-latency inference, and knowledgedriven response, making it a promising step toward scalable and intelligent cybersecurity management in 6G-enabled IoT networks. INDEX TERMS Security, agentic AI, attack mitigation, autonomous cyber defense, intrusion prediction, LLMs, SLMs, IoT. I. INTRODUCTION T he Internet of Things (IoT) has experienced exponential growth, from consumer applications to critical sectors such as healthcare, defense, and vehicular networks. As IoT evolves toward 6G-enabled networks, massive connectivity, edge intelligence, and ultra-low latency further accelerate this transformation. However, they also broaden the attack surface and shrink the response window [1]. Securing such dynamic environments, therefore, demands cybersecurity mechanisms that are accurate, eﬃcient, adaptive, and capable of coordinated real-time response. Recent reports indicate more than 820,000 daily IoT attack attempts in 2025, along with a 46% increase in malware year-over-year [2]. Massive botnets, such as Aisuru, have launched record-breaking 29.7 Tbps Distributed Denial-ofService (DDoS) attacks, while insecure endpoints contribute to 40% of global DDoS traﬃc [3], [4]. These challenges are further ampliﬁed in dynamic environments such as vehicular 10178 © 2026 The Authors. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License. For more information, see https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ VOLUME 7, 2026 networks and emerging 6G-enabled IoT networks, where rapid topological changes and resource constraints render conventional reactive defenses insuﬃcient [5]. Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) are widely deployed to monitor network activity and identify potential threats in IoT environments. However, most IDS operate reactively at the ﬂow level [6], [7], detecting attacks only after traﬃc aggregation introduces latency. This delay is problematic for fast-evolving threats such as ransomware and advanced persistent threats (APTs) [8]. Packet-level approaches provide ﬁne-grained visibility and enable earlier detection of attack indicators, but face scalability challenges in large-scale deployments [9]. In contrast, ﬂow-level methods improve scalability at the expense of delayed detection [10]. Moreover, most existing IDSs lack integrated mitigation capabilities and remain challenged by resource-constrained IoT environments [11], [12], leaving dynamic networks such as the Internet-of-Vehicles (IoV) particularly vulnerable [13]. The transition toward 6G-enabled IoT networks further ampliﬁes cybersecurity challenges due to ultra-low latency requirements, massive device density, highly dynamic network topologies, and distributed edge computing infrastructures. In such environments, conventional reactive intrusion detection mechanisms become increasingly insuﬃcient, as delayed responses may allow attacks to rapidly propagate across interconnected devices and edge nodes. Consequently, 6G-enabled IoT networks require proactive and autonomous cybersecurity frameworks capable of continuously monitoring traﬃc behavior, anticipating threats before complete attack manifestation, and supporting low-latency adaptive response mechanisms [14]. To address these limitations, recent research [15], [16], [17] has moved toward proactive security approaches that seek to anticipate cyber threats before their occurrence. Although promising, most existing solutions remain limited in scope and primarily focus on isolated prediction tasks, without supporting coordinated decision-making or integrated response mechanisms. In highly dynamic IoT environments, eﬀective defense requires intelligent systems that can continuously monitor network behavior, anticipate emerging threats, and adapt their decisions according to evolving traﬃc patterns [18]. While recent proactive IDS approaches have leveraged machine learning (ML) to anticipate attacks [15], [19], they often struggle to capture long-range temporal dependencies and subtle contextual patterns in network traﬃc. Alternatively, Transformer-based Large Language Models (LLMs), which leverage self-attention mechanisms to process sequential data, oﬀer a promising approach for modeling complex temporal dependencies in network traﬃc. Recent studies [16], [17], [20], [21], [22] have explored their use for proactive intrusion prediction, demonstrating the ability to detect early indicators of malicious activity. However, the high computational and memory requirements of full-scale LLMs limit their applicability in IoT environments. Small Language Models (SLMs) address this challenge by retaining much of the predictive capability of LLMs while being lightweight and resource-eﬃcient [23], [24]. Their compact design enables frequent model updates, low-latency inference, and scalable operation across distributed and resource-constrained networks. Importantly, SLMs provide an eﬃcient foundation for role-specialized cybersecurity systems capable of continuously monitoring network behavior, anticipating threats, and supporting low-latency decisionmaking. These capabilities support emerging role-based multi-agent cybersecurity paradigms that leverage structured coordination and knowledge-guided decision-making to enable proactive and context-aware defense in complex IoT networks [25], [26]. In line with the discussion above, we propose a rolebased multi-agent framework for proactive cyber defense and cooperative response in IoT networks. The proposed framework integrates prediction, detection, and mitigation within a uniﬁed and coordinated network architecture. It leverages SLMs for packet-level intrusion forecasting, a LightGBM-based classiﬁer for real-time detection, and a D3FEND-aligned knowledge graph for knowledge-driven threat mitigation recommendation. The main contributions can be summarized as follows: 1) We design a coordinated multi-agent framework in which prediction, detection, and mitigation agents operate collaboratively, ensuring continuous monitoring, adaptive decision-making, and proactive protection. Speciﬁcally: 2) We propose a proactive intrusion forecasting mechanism that leverages SLMs to predict malicious activity at the packet level, before a network ﬂow is fully completed. 3) We develop a real-time detection system based on a resource-eﬃcient LightGBM classiﬁer. It performs traﬃc classiﬁcation on the ﬂy, oﬀering low latency and scalability for IoT and edge environments. 4) We introduce a knowledge-driven mitigation component that exploits semantic knowledge from the D3FENDTM framework. The system maps detected threats to appropriate countermeasures, supporting structured mitigation recommendation generation. 5) We validate the eﬃcacy of our approach on the large-scale CICIoT2023 dataset. Experimental results demonstrate early threat detection, high classiﬁcation accuracy, and practical feasibility in real-world IoT environments, compared to benchmarks. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II overviews the related work, while Section III describes the proposed framework. Section IV presents the experimental setup and datasets, and Section V discusses the results along with a benchmarking against state-of-the-art methods. Finally, Section VI concludes the paper. VOLUME 7, 2026 10179 DIAF et al.: LIGHTWEIGHT LANGUAGE-MODEL-DRIVEN AGENTIC FRAMEWORK II. RELATED WORK This section reviews recent advances in intrusion detection, prediction, and mitigation in IoT networks, with a focus on AI-driven approaches and their limitations in enabling proactive and coordinated cybersecurity. IDSs are necessary to secure IoT networks by monitoring traﬃc and identifying malicious activities. However, the heterogeneous, large-scale, and dynamic nature of IoT environments poses signiﬁcant challenges for traditional IDS, particularly in achieving real-time detection, adapting to evolving threats, and providing integrated mitigation capabilities [27]. The latter have evolved from signature-based detection toward anomaly detection using classical ML algorithms such as Decision Trees, Random Forests, K-Nearest Neighbors, and Logistic Regression [28]. Ensemble models, such as LightGBM, further demonstrated high detection accuracy with low computational overhead in IoT networks [29]. Although they enable eﬃcient traﬃc classiﬁcation, these methods’ ability to capture complex and evolving attack patterns remains limited. Deep learning (DL) models have been increasingly adopted since then to automatically learn discriminative representations from network traﬃc. Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), including Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), capture spatial and temporal patterns, enabling more eﬀective IoT intrusion detection [28], [30], [31]. In parallel, recent work showed that combining ML with feature engineering enhances intrusion detection, in terms of accuracy and false positive events, when using resource-constrained IoT protocols such as MQTT [32]. More recently, hybrid DL architectures and Transformer-based models have been explored to model complex relationships in network traﬃc using attention mechanisms [33]. However, these approaches often require large training datasets and substantial computational resources, limiting their applicability in real-world 6G-enabled IoT networks. Also, most existing IDS remain inherently reactive, detecting malicious activity only after suﬃcient attack patterns have manifested in network traﬃc [34]. Alternatively, generative AI has been explored for cybersecurity in 6G-enabled IoT networks, demonstrating promising intrusion detection capabilities for evolving threat scenarios [35]. Similarly, combined DL with optimization algorithms achieved high detection accuracy while supporting eﬃcient threat detection [36]. Within the last few years, LLM-based hybrid architectures have been proposed. For instance, GPT–BERT–LSTM frameworks [16], LLM agents [37], and BARTPredict [17] have been proposed to forecast traﬃc for early attack detection. Given their computation overhead, SLMs are seen as an interesting alternative for deployment at the edge of the network. For example, lightweight BERT variants (DistilBERT, MobileBERT, and TinyBERT) enabled eﬃcient attack detection inference with reduced resource requirements [38], [39], [40]. Moreover, SLMs are increasingly recognized as a promising component of agentic AI [25], highlighting their potential role in coordinated and knowledge-driven cybersecurity systems. While predictive models provide foresight into potential attacks, eﬀective cybersecurity in 6G-enabled IoT environments requires coordinated mechanisms for real-time response and mitigation support. In fact, traditional mitigation strategies, based on rule-based ﬁrewalls and static segmentation, struggle to adapt to evolving threats and the dynamic nature of IoT networks [41]. In parallel, reinforcement learning (RL) has been explored for adaptive mitigation, where agents learn optimal defense policies through simulated interactions [42], [43]. Multi-agent RL (MARL) frameworks have further enabled coordinated distributed responses across IoT nodes, demonstrating resilience against various attack scenarios [44], [45], [46]. Nevertheless, these approaches were computationally intensive and often exhibited limited eﬃciency–performance trade-oﬀs. Existing studies mainly focused on isolated aspects of cybersecurity in 6G-enabled IoT networks, such as traﬃc prediction, intrusion detection, or knowledge-driven mitigation, often treating these tasks independently. However, recent studies emphasized that proactive threat identiﬁcation, adaptive mitigation strategies, and self-sustaining network operation are key requirements for future end-to-end 6G network automation [14]. In this context, the proposed framework integrates packet-level traﬃc prediction, ﬂow-level intrusion detection, and mitigation recommendation generation within a coordinated multi-agent architecture. Moreover, unlike computationally intensive RL- and MARL-based defense approaches [47], [48], our framework leverages SLM-driven prediction and knowledge-guided mitigation mechanisms to enable proactive cybersecurity while maintaining computational eﬃciency for large-scale IoT environments. III. PROPOSED FRAMEWORK This section presents our multi-agent framework, which comprises three complementary autonomous agents that collaboratively perform packet forecasting, intrusion detection, and knowledge-driven mitigation. These agents interact through an agent coordination layer responsible for task orchestration, information exchange, and contextual preservation across the prediction–detection–mitigation cycle. Fig. 1 illustrates the overall architecture and inter-agent data ﬂows. A. FRAMEWORK OVERVIEW AND DATA REPRESENTATION The proposed architecture is structured around three specialized agents: a Prediction Agent, a Detection Agent, and a Mitigation Agent. Through the coordination layer, they operate as a uniﬁed system that enables continuous decision ﬂow and adaptive feedback. As illustrated in Fig. 1, data ﬂows sequentially from the Prediction Agent to the Detection Agent and then to the Mitigation Agent, with feedback loops supporting coordinated decision propagation across the framework. Table 1 complements this architectural view by 10180 VOLUME 7, 2026 FIGURE 1. Pipeline of the proposed multi-agent framework. The Prediction Agent forecasts future packets using SLMs, the Detection Agent combines packet-level and ﬂow-level analysis for proactive intrusion detection, and the Mitigation Agent maps detected attacks to D3FEND-aligned countermeasures through coordinated agent interaction. TABLE 1. Summary of Agent Roles, Inputs, Outputs, and Key Tasks in the Proposed Framework. summarizing the inputs, outputs, primary roles, and key tasks of each agent and the coordination layer. At a high level, the framework operates in three sequential stages. First, the Prediction Agent observes a history of packets belonging to a given network ﬂow and forecasts the next packet, while validating its plausibility against prior context to ﬁlter inconsistent predictions. Second, the Detection Agent processes both the forecasted packet and real-time ﬂow data, combining ﬂow-level classiﬁcation for high-conﬁdence detection with packet-level analysis enriched by ﬂow context for early threat identiﬁcation. Third, upon detecting malicious activity, the Mitigation Agent maps the attack category to MITRE ATT&CK techniques and retrieves appropriate countermeasures from the D3FEND knowledge graph. This design yields a uniﬁed, lightweight, and proactive defense strategy suitable for dynamic and resource-constrained 6G-enabled IoT environments. To support agent reasoning, network traﬃc is represented through structured packet-level and ﬂow-level datasets that preserve temporal dependencies and protocol semantics, serving as a common input space for both prediction and detection. 1) PACKET-LEVEL REPRESENTATION FOR PREDICTIONS IoT network communications are modeled as ordered packet sequences associated with individual traﬃc ﬂows. Instead VOLUME 7, 2026 10181 DIAF et al.: LIGHTWEIGHT LANGUAGE-MODEL-DRIVEN AGENTIC FRAMEWORK detection on forecasted packets. Each instance combines packet-level features with rolling ﬂow statistics computed only from past packets, ensuring temporal causality. - D2C: a ﬂow-classiﬁcation dataset used for real-time intrusion detection. Each ﬂow is summarized by 65 aggregated features capturing temporal statistics (e.g., inter-arrival times, packet size variations), TCP characteristics (window sizes, roundtrip-time (RTT), sequence/acknowledgment behavior), and IP/ICMP metrics (time-to-live (TTL), options, echo ratios). These representations are designed to maintain low computational complexity while retaining suﬃcient discriminative power for high-accuracy detection. FIGURE 2. Textual packet-ﬂow representation used in the DF forecasting dataset. Each packet is transformed into a sequence of feature tokens separated by [SEP] markers, with [BoF] (resp. [EoF]) denoting the start (resp. end) of the ﬂow. of analyzing packets independently or relying solely on aggregated ﬂow statistics, our framework preserves the chronological progression of packets within each connection to capture dependencies. This choice is motivated by the observation that attack patterns often manifest as speciﬁc sequences of packet headers, ﬂags, or timing anomalies that are only detectable when temporal context is preserved. Formally, an “observed traﬃc ﬂow” (See the Input in Fig. 1) is represented as F = (P1, P2, . . . , Pn), where each packet Pi is encoded as a feature vector Pi = φ(pi) ∈ Rk, with φ(·) denotes a feature extraction function applied to the raw packet pi. The extracted attributes capture protocol information (e.g., TCP ﬂags, IP options), timing dynamics (inter-arrival times), and transport-layer semantics derived from layers 2–4 of the TCP/IP stack. We deliberately exclude payload content to maintain lightweight processing and preserve privacy. This sequential formulation enables autoregressive modeling of packet streams. Concretely, the forecasting task estimates the conditional probability of the next packet given its preceding context: P(Pt+1 | P1, . . . , Pt). We then transform packet sequences into a tokenized textual representation suitable for ﬁne-tuning SLMs, as illustrated in Fig. 2, and where we explicitly indicate ﬂow boundaries by inserting sentinel markers [BoF] (beginning-of-ﬂow) and [EoF] (end-of-ﬂow). The parsing stage produces two packet-level datasets: DF for next-packet forecasting and DV for packet-pair validation, the latter being used to verify the consistency between consecutive packets. Importantly, dataset splits are performed at the ﬂow level to ensure that temporally correlated samples remain within the same subset (training, validation, or testing), thereby preventing any data leakage between splits. 2) FLOW-LEVEL AND PACKET-LEVEL REPRESENTATIONS FOR DETECTION The Detection Agent relies on both packet-level and ﬂowlevel representations. We construct two datasets accordingly: - D1C: a packet-classiﬁcation dataset used for proactive B. PREDICTION AGENT Building on the packet-level representation introduced above, the “Prediction Agent” anticipates future network traﬃc by modeling the temporal evolution of packets within each ﬂow. It combines complementary tasks: autoregressive nextpacket forecasting, packet-pair consistency validation, and proactive intrusion prediction through forecast-driven analysis of malicious activity before ﬂow completion. In this context, packet forecasting refers to generating future packet representations from historical sequences, while proactive intrusion prediction and detection refer to identifying malicious behavior based on these forecasted packets prior to full ﬂow observation. The rationale for adding validation is that raw autoregressive predictions may occasionally violate protocol semantics (e.g., an impossible TCP ﬂag transition). By ﬁltering predictions through a bidirectional validation model, we increase the reliability of the forecasted packets before they are passed to the Detection Agent. Both tasks leverage the Transformer architecture and its attention mechanism [49]. Given query, key, and value matrices Q, K, and V, attention is computed as Attention(Q, K, V ) = softmax Â QK √ Ã V (1) dk where dk denotes the dimensionality of the key vectors. This allows the SLM model to dynamically focus on the most relevant packets within the sequence when computing contextual representations. 1) NEXT-PACKET FORECASTING Given a packet sequence F = (P1, . . ., Pt), the forecasting task predicts the next packet Pˆt+1 as Pˆt+1 = fθ(P1, . . ., Pt), where fθ denotes an autoregressive SLM parameterized by θ. We ﬁne-tune the DistilGPT2 SLM [50] using the dataset DF. The input sequences are tokenized packet representations, and the model is trained to minimize the causal language modeling loss: N X LGPT = − log P(xk | x1, . . ., xk−1; θ). (2) k=1 Through this autoregressive learning process, the model captures temporal dependencies and protocol dynamics across packet sequences. Unlike ﬂow-level methods that wait for ﬂow completion, our forecasting agent can anticipate the 10182 VOLUME 7, 2026 next packet after only a few preceding packets, enabling early detection. 2) PACKET-PAIR CONSISTENCY VALIDATION To ensure that a forecasted packet Pˆt+1 is plausible given the actual preceding packet Pt, we train a binary classiﬁer on the dataset DV . Each sample consists of a packet pair (Pt, Pt+1) labeled as valid (1) if the pair appears consecutively in the same ﬂow, or invalid (0) otherwise. Invalid pairs are generated by randomly shuﬄing packets from diﬀerent ﬂows or by introducing protocol violations. We implement this classiﬁer by ﬁne-tuning DistilBERT [24], a lightweight bidirectional SLM. The model takes the concatenated representation of the packet pair and outputs a validity probability: X LPE = log P(y | X; θ) (3) (X,y) where X denotes the packet-pair representation, y ∈ {0, 1} is the ground-truth label, and θ represents the model parameters. By leveraging bidirectional contextual representations, the validation model can detect protocol inconsistencies (e.g., a SYN packet following a FIN packet without handshake), anomalous transitions, or semantic violations that may arise during autoregressive prediction. Only forecasted packets that satisfy this validation step (i.e., vt ≥ τv, where τv is a conﬁdence threshold) are forwarded to the Detection Agent. In our implementation, the threshold τv is set to the default decision boundary of the binary DistilBERT classiﬁer (τv = 0.5). Consequently, forecasted packets with vt ≥ 0.5 are classiﬁed as valid and forwarded to the Detection Agent, whereas those with vt < 0.5 are classiﬁed as invalid and discarded. To ensure consistency and reproducibility, the same threshold was used throughout all experiments without further adjustment. This ﬁltering mechanism helps ensure that only semantically consistent forecasted packets are considered during proactive detection. C. DETECTION AGENT The “Detection Agent” analyzes both forecasted packets (after validation) and real-time network ﬂows to identify malicious activities. It combines two perspectives: packetlevel inspection for early warning, and ﬂow-level monitoring for robust baseline detection. 1) FLOW-LEVEL CLASSIFICATION We train a lightweight Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM) [51] classiﬁer on the ﬂow-level dataset D2C. Each ﬂow F = (P1, . . ., Pn) is summarized into a feature vector of 65 aggregated statistics, including temporal moments, TCP ﬂags distribution, window size evolution, and ICMP echo ratios. LightGBM is chosen for its high eﬃciency, low memory footprint, and ability to handle mixed categorical-numerical features. Flow-level analysis provides an initial assessment of the traﬃc (as an attack or benign). 2) PROACTIVE PACKET-LEVEL CLASSIFICATION WITH FLOW CONTEXT For proactive detection on forecasted packets Pˆt+1, we enrich the packet-level classiﬁer with ﬂow context. The motivation is that individual packet features alone are often insuﬃcient to discriminate malicious from benign traﬃc, especially in the early stage of an attack. We therefore construct a hybrid feature vector: xhybrid, t+1 = [xpacket, t+1, φ(Ft), yˆﬂow, t], where: - xpacket, t+1 ∈ RN comprises packet-level features extracted from Pˆt+1, where N denotes the number of features (e.g., protocol ﬁelds, ﬂags, lengths); - φ(Ft) captures ﬂowlevel statistics from recent traﬃc up to time t, such as rolling means, variances, and min/max values of packet sizes and inter-arrival times (strict temporal causality); - yˆﬂow, t ∈ RC denotes the class probability vector output by the ﬂow-level classiﬁer at time t, providing a soft behavioral context. We then train a second LightGBM classiﬁer on this enriched feature space using the dataset D1C. The model maps the hybrid representation to a packet-level prediction: M X yˆpacket, t+1 = fm(xhybrid, t+1) (4) m=1 where fm ∈ F are regression trees in the ensemble, and yˆpacket, t+1 denotes the predicted packet-level label at time t + 1. The ﬁnal prediction is obtained by aggregating the outputs of all trees in the ensemble. Each tree fm minimizes the residual loss: N X fm = arg min L f ∈F yi, yˆ(im−1) + f (xh(iy)brid)Á (5) i=1 where: - yi denotes the ground-truth label for sample i, - yˆi(m−1) denotes the ensemble prediction at iteration m − 1, - xh(iy)brid denotes the hybrid feature vector for sample i. Algorithm 1 Proactive Packet Classiﬁcation Require: Forecasted packet Pˆt+1, current ﬂow Ft Ensure: Proactive class prediction yˆpacket, t+1 1: xpacket, t+1 ← φp(Pˆ t+1) 2: ct ← φc(Ft) 3: if yˆﬂow, t is not available then 4: yˆﬂow, t ← qγ(φ f (Ft)) 5: end if 6: xhybrid, t+1 ← [xpacket, t+1, ct, yˆﬂow, t] 7: yˆpacket, t+1 ← hω(xhybrid, t+1) 8: return yˆpacket, t+1 This hybrid approach allows the packet classiﬁer to beneﬁt from the behavioral richness of ﬂow analysis while preserving the low latency of packet-level inspection. As summarized in Algorithm 1, the procedure ﬁrst extracts packet-level features from the forecasted packet together with contextual information from the current ﬂow (Lines 1–2). If a ﬂow-level prediction is not already available, the ﬂow VOLUME 7, 2026 10183 DIAF et al.: LIGHTWEIGHT LANGUAGE-MODEL-DRIVEN AGENTIC FRAMEWORK classiﬁer is executed to obtain the corresponding behavioral context (Lines 3–5). These packet features, ﬂow statistics, and ﬂow-level predictions are then combined into a hybrid representation (Line 6), which is processed by the LightGBM classiﬁer to generate the proactive attack prediction (Line 7). D. MITIGATION AGENT AND END-TO-END COORDINATION The “Mitigation Agent” is responsible for translating detection alerts into structured defense recommendations. Rather than relying on hard-coded rules, we adopt a knowledgedriven approach that leverages two widely adopted cybersecurity ontologies: MITRE ATT&CK for adversarial behavior description and MITRE D3FEND for countermeasure mapping. The agent operates in two steps. First, given a detected alert at ∈ A (e.g., “DDoS-UDP Flood”), we map it to one or more ATT&CK techniques tt using a predeﬁned lookup table derived from the CICIoT2023 attack taxonomy. For example, a UDP ﬂood is mapped to technique T1498 (Network Denial of Service). Second, we query the D3FEND knowledge graph [52] using tt as the entry point, retrieving a set of countermeasures Rt. These countermeasures fall into categories such as Detect, Harden, Isolate, Deceive, and Evict. The agent then outputs a recommended defense action, e.g., “rate limiting” or “connection reset”, intended to support security analysts or downstream enforcement systems. The proposed framework does not directly execute mitigation actions; rather, it generates structured mitigation recommendations that can be reviewed and enforced by external security platforms, network administrators, or automated response systems. Formally, the mitigation process is: at −−M−→1 tt −−M−→2 Rt, where M1 is the attack-to-technique mapping and M2 is the D3FEND retrieval function. The complete framework operates as a coordinated pipeline. Given packet history St = (P1, . . ., Pt) and the current ﬂow Ft, the Prediction Agent generates a forecasted packet Pˆt+1 using the SLM model fθ and validates it with the DistilBERT model gψ to obtain a validity score vt. If vt exceeds threshold τv, we proceed to proactive detection; otherwise, the forecast is discarded, and no proactive alert is raised. Meanwhile, ﬂow-level detection runs independently, producing yˆﬂow, t. For valid forecasts, we construct the hybrid feature vector xhybrid, t+1 and obtain a proactive packet-level prediction yˆpacket, t+1 using the LightGBM classiﬁer hω. The ﬁnal alert at is determined using a logical OR fusion rule between ﬂow-level and packet-level predictions. Speciﬁcally, an alert is raised if either the ﬂow-level classiﬁer predicts a non-benign class or the packet-level classiﬁer identiﬁes malicious activity from the forecasted packet. If an alert is raised (i.e., at Benign), the Mitigation Agent maps it to ATT&CK techniques tt and retrieves the corresponding countermeasures Rt. Otherwise, an empty mitigation set is returned. Algorithm 2 summarizes the end-to-end Algorithm 2 Prediction–Detection–Mitigation Workﬂow Require: Packet history St = (P1, . . ., Pt), real-time ﬂow Ft Ensure: Final alert at, mitigation set Rt 1: Pˆ t+1 ← fθ(St) 2: vt ← gψ(Pt, Pˆ t+1) 3: yˆﬂow, t ← qγ(φ f (Ft)) 4: if vt ≥ τv then 5: xhybrid, t+1 ← [φp(Pˆ t+1), φc(Ft), yˆﬂow, t] 6: yˆpacket, t+1 ← hω(xhybrid, t+1) 7: else 8: yˆpacket, t+1 ← ∅ 9: end if 10: at ← D(yˆpacket, t+1, yˆﬂow, t) 11: if at Benign then 12: tt ← M1(at) 13: Rt ← M2(tt) 14: else 15: Rt ← ∅ 16: end if 17: return (at, Rt) coordination among the framework agents. The Prediction Agent ﬁrst generates and validates a forecasted packet, while the ﬂow-level detector analyzes the current traﬃc ﬂow (Lines 1–3). If the forecast is considered valid, proactive packet-level classiﬁcation is performed using the hybrid feature representation (Lines 4–9). The packet-level and ﬂow-level decisions are then fused to produce a ﬁnal alert (Line 10). When malicious activity is detected, the Mitigation Agent maps the alert to ATT&CK techniques and retrieves the corresponding D3FEND countermeasures (Lines 11–15). Finally, the framework outputs the generated alert together with the corresponding mitigation recommendations (Line 17). Our design ensures that the framework closes the loop from prediction to mitigation recommendation by generating structured defense guidance derived from ATT&CK–D3FEND reasoning. The framework focuses on recommendation generation rather than autonomous execution of defensive actions. IV. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP This section describes the experimental methodology for evaluating the proposed agentic framework, including dataset preparation, ﬂow extraction, and model training. A. PACKET AND FLOW DATASET PREPARATION We select the CICIoT2023 dataset [53] due to its large-scale and recent representation of IoT network traﬃc, including both benign and diverse malicious behaviors generated from realistic IoT environments. The dataset provides heterogeneous attack scenarios and rich traﬃc patterns, making it well-suited for evaluating intrusion prediction and classiﬁcation models in IoT settings. 10184 VOLUME 7, 2026 TABLE 2. Selected attack classes from the CICIoT2023 dataset. In this study, we consider a subset of attack classes covering several key IoT threat categories. Speciﬁcally, the selected classes include major IoT attack families targeted by the proposed framework, namely Denial-of-Service (DoS), Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS), spooﬁng, and reconnaissance attacks. The latter were chosen since they represent prevalent and operationally signiﬁcant threats in IoT environments, and exhibit varied behavioral characteristics relevant to IoT threat modeling. Moreover, the inclusion of both volumetric ﬂooding attacks and stealth-oriented activities enables assessment under diﬀerent attack dynamics and operational conditions. The selected classes and their corresponding categories are summarized in Table 2. The dataset sizes and class-wise distributions for both benign and attack categories are summarized in Table 6, with further details on the constructed subsets provided in the following subsections. For packet-level processing, we parse packets from the raw CICIoT2023 PCAP traﬃc using the packet-forensics mode of the Tranalyzer traﬃc exporter [54]. This process extracts protocol ﬁelds from layers 2–4 of the TCP/IP stack. Features with low variance or high correlation are removed to reduce redundancy, and malformed or empty rows are discarded to ensure data consistency. The resulting packet sequences are tokenized and delimited using sentinel markers [BoF] and [EoF] to explicitly indicate ﬂow boundaries for GPT-based next-packet forecasting. In parallel, we construct ﬂow-level datasets from the CICIoT2023 PCAP ﬁles where packets are grouped into ﬂows using the standard 5-tuple identiﬁcation (source IP, destination IP, source port, destination port, and transport protocol). For each ﬂow, temporal, statistical, and protocol-based features are aggregated across all packets, forming structured ﬂow representations to be used for LightGBM-based ﬂowlevel classiﬁcation. The standard 5-tuple is also adopted for ﬂow-consistent splitting. Speciﬁcally, all packets belonging to the same ﬂow are assigned to the same subset (training, validation, or testing) to prevent data leakage across temporally correlated samples. The dataset is split into 70% training and 30% testing at the ﬂow level, with packet-level random partitioning avoided to ensure ﬂow consistency. This ﬂowlevel partitioning is consistently applied across all datasets used by the prediction, detection, and validation agents. From the processed traﬃc data, three datasets are constructed to support the diﬀerent agents of the framework: • Packet Forecasting Dataset (DF): Ordered packet sequences delimited with [BoF] and [EoF] markers, used for ﬁne-tuning the GPT-based next-packet forecasting model. • Prediction Validation Dataset (DV ): Packet pairs labeled as consecutive or non-consecutive, used to train the DistilBERT-based packet-pair validation model. • Packet & Flow Classiﬁcation Dataset (DC): Normalized and encoded packet and ﬂow feature representations supporting multi-class intrusion classiﬁcation using LightGBM. In addition, a separate Forecasted Packet dataset is built from GPT predictions to evaluate the proactive detection capability of the framework. For proactive detection, the packet classiﬁer uses a hybrid 46-dimensional feature space combining 25 Tranalyzer-extracted packet features, 8 rolling ﬂow aggregates (mean and standard deviation over a sliding window of recent packets, computed from historical packets only), and 13 ﬂow model predictions providing behavioral context. This design ensures temporal causality, as only previously observed packets contribute to the context for predicting future packets. For real-time detection, we extract 65 aggregated ﬂow features from complete ﬂows, capturing temporal statistics (inter-arrival times, packet sizes), TCP characteristics (window sizes, round-trip-time (RTT), sequence/acknowledgment behavior), and IP/ICMP metrics (time-to-live (TTL), options, echo ratios). The complete feature set for both detection modes is summarized in Table 3. B. AGENTIC ORCHESTRATION FRAMEWORK We conducted all experiments using Kaggle notebooks equipped with two Tesla T4 GPUs (16 GB VRAM each) and 29 GB of system memory. The proposed framework employs a role-based multi-agent orchestration layer implemented using the CrewAI framework [55] (v1.10.1), a lightweight Python framework for coordinating role-specialized AI agents through structured task-driven Crew workﬂows. The orchestration layer coordinates forecasting, detection, and mitigation agents. LLAMA 3.2 1B (Ollama runtime) supports reasoning within each agent’s deﬁned scope, assisting with detection outcome interpretation, decision coordination, and mitigation recommendation generation. Three specialized agents operate within this framework: (i) a Prediction Agent for next-packet forecasting and validation, (ii) a Detection Agent for intrusion detection and attack classiﬁcation, and (iii) a Mitigation Agent deriving D3FEND-based countermeasures. Each agent operates within a constrained functional scope deﬁned by its assigned task and the underlying ATT&CK–D3FEND knowledge structure. This design emphasizes decomposition of the cybersecurity workﬂow into interacting specialized agents coordinated through structured reasoning and information exchange, rather than unrestricted autonomous reasoning. VOLUME 7, 2026 10185 DIAF et al.: LIGHTWEIGHT LANGUAGE-MODEL-DRIVEN AGENTIC FRAMEWORK TABLE 3. Original Feature Sets for Packet-Level and Flow-Level Detection. TABLE 4. Prediction Agent Dataset Distribution. TABLE 5. Hyperparameters for Prediction Agent Models. C. PREDICTION AGENT: FORECASTING AND VALIDATION The Prediction Agent is responsible for modeling packet sequence dynamics and validating predicted packet transitions. It consists of two complementary tasks: (i) next-packet forecasting and (ii) packet-pair validation. We implemented both models using the Hugging Face transformers library [56]. Table 4 summarizes the dataset sizes used for next-packet forecasting and packet-pair validation. The packet forecasting dataset is approximately balanced across traﬃc classes, with train ﬂows per class ranging from 5,202 to 15,000, and inference ﬂows ranging from 2,231 to 5,000. Next-Packet Forecasting Task: We ﬁne-tune the DISTILGPT2 model on the Packet Forecasting Dataset (DF) to perform the forecasting task. The model learns to predict the next packet Pˆt+1 given a sequence of packets (P1, . . ., Pt) using causal language modeling. We represented packet sequences in textual form and delimited with the sentinel markers [BoF] and [EoF]. Packet-Pair Validation Task: To verify the coherence of predicted packet transitions, we trained a DISTILBERT model on the Prediction Evaluation Dataset (DV ). The dataset contains packet pairs labeled as consecutive or nonconsecutive. The model learns to classify whether a predicted packet logically follows the previous packet within the traﬃc sequence or not. The main hyperparameters used for ﬁne-tuning both models are summarized in Table 5. They were selected to balance between training stability and computational eﬃciency. D. DETECTION AGENT: HYBRID PACKET-FLOW CLASSIFICATION The Detection Agent employs a dual-model design reﬂecting the complementary strengths of packet and ﬂow analysis. TABLE 6. Detection Agent Dataset Distribution. We train the ﬂow model on the ﬂow-level dataset D2C, which aggregates temporal and protocol-based features across complete ﬂows. For proactive detection, we train the packet classiﬁer on D1C with its feature space extended using ﬂow context, following the hybrid feature construction described in Subsection IV-A. Both datasets follow a 70% training, 30% testing split, with class distribution summarized in Table 6. We use a separate forecasted packet dataset of 65,000 samples (5,000 per class) to evaluate proactive detection performance. We determine LightGBM hyperparameters empirically to balance model complexity and generalization. The ﬂow model uses 95 leaves, a maximum depth of 8, 300 estimators, 10186 VOLUME 7, 2026 and L1/L2 regularization of 0.1. The packet classiﬁer leverages a lighter conﬁguration with 31 leaves, depth 7, 1500 estimators, lighter regularization (0.08 for L1/L2), subsampling at 0.85 for both samples and features, and early stopping. We evaluate our models using standard classiﬁcation metrics, i.e., Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and F1-score, computed from true positives (TP), true negatives (TN), false positives (FP), and false negatives (FN) as Accuracy = TP + TN TP + TN + FP + FN Precision = T P/(T P + FP) Recall = T P/(T P + FN) F1-score = 2 × Precision Precision × + Recall Recall (6) (7) FIGURE 3. Confusion Matrix for Packet-Pair Classiﬁcation. (8) (9) In addition to these standard classiﬁcation metrics, we introduce a system-level evaluation metric to quantify the temporal advantage of the proposed proactive detection mechanism. Speciﬁcally, we deﬁne a detection ratio that measures the fraction of a malicious ﬂow required before an alert can be raised, given by: Rd = Nc , Lf (10) where Nc denotes the minimum packet context required before proactive prediction can be triggered, and L f represents the total number of packets in the malicious ﬂow. This metric quantiﬁes the fraction of a ﬂow required before an attack can be identiﬁed, where lower values indicate earlier detection relative to full-ﬂow analysis. FIGURE 4. Acceptance rate improvement of forecasted packets across attack categories over iterative optimization cycles: initial prediction (Init.), after the 1st optimization cycle (Opt. 1), and after the 2nd optimization cycle (Opt. 2). E. MITIGATION AGENT: D3FEND-BASED COUNTERMEASURES The Mitigation Agent recommends defensive actions for detected threats using the MITRE D3FEND knowledge graph [52]. To ensure standardized reasoning, detected attack classes are ﬁrst assigned to MITRE ATT&CK techniques, which serve as an intermediate representation of adversarial behavior. In this work, we use D3FEND version 1.3.0, which contains over 874 defensive artifacts. The system is designed to query the D3FEND knowledge graph via its API or a local ontology representation to ensure ﬂexibility and reproducibility. D3FEND groups defensive techniques into strategy categories such as Detect, Harden, Isolate, Deceive, and Evict. Once an attack is detected, the Mitigation Agent retrieves relevant countermeasures from the D3FEND knowledge graph and outputs corresponding defensive actions. V. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION This section presents our experimental results evaluating the Prediction Agent (Subsection V-A), the proactive and realtime Detection Agent (Subsection V-B), and the Mitigation Agent (Subsection V-C). A. PREDICTION/FORECASTING PERFORMANCE This subsection evaluates the Prediction Agent’s next-packet forecasting and packet-pair validation performances. First, we present in Fig. 3 the confusion matrix of the DistilBERT-based Packet Pair Classiﬁer (PPC), which classiﬁes packet pairs as successive or non-successive. It achieves an average accuracy of 97.44%, succeeding in well-distinguishing between successive and non-successive packets. A feedback-driven reﬁnement process is then employed, where forecasted packets generated by DistilGPT2 are iteratively validated and used to guide its optimization, with validation feedback across cycles driving both the selection of additional ﬁne-tuning data and the re-optimization of model hyperparameters. As shown in Fig. 4, the acceptance rate of forecasted packets, deﬁned as the proportion of predicted packet pairs classiﬁed as valid successive pairs, increases signiﬁcantly over three optimization cycles. Starting from 25.74% for the initial prediction, the acceptance rate improves to 77.60% after the ﬁrst optimization cycle, and reaches 99.90% after the ﬁnal optimization cycle. Each cycle incorporates feedback from the PPC to correct inconsistencies such as protocol violations and weak temporal VOLUME 7, 2026 10187 DIAF et al.: LIGHTWEIGHT LANGUAGE-MODEL-DRIVEN AGENTIC FRAMEWORK FIGURE 5. Confusion matrices for real-time ﬂow detection and proactive packet detection, with and without ﬂow context. TABLE 7. Packet-Pair Validation Performance. TABLE 8. Proactive Detection Performance: Real vs Forecasted Packets. dependencies, progressively enhancing prediction quality. This iterative improvement highlights the eﬀectiveness of feedback-guided optimization in producing temporally consistent and valid packet sequences. B. PROACTIVE AND REAL-TIME DETECTION PERFORMANCE In Fig. 5, we present the confusion matrices for three detection settings: (a) proactive packet-level detection with ﬂow context, (b) proactive detection without ﬂow context, and (c) real-time ﬂow-based detection. Accordingly, we see in Fig. 5a that proactive detection with ﬂow context achieves a strong classiﬁcation performance, with an average accuracy of 98.24%. The matrix exhibits clear diagonal dominance, indicating that most traﬃc categories are reliably distinguished at the packet level. Residual errors are primarily concentrated between benign and spooﬁng traﬃc, and between UDP-based DoS and DDoS attacks (DDoS-UDP Flood vs. DoS-UDP Flood). These classes remain more challenging to distinguish because they exhibit highly similar packet-level characteristics. In particular, spooﬁng attacks often preserve many legitimate protocol characteristics while manipulating identity-related information, making them diﬃcult to distinguish from benign traﬃc based solely on individual packet observations. Similarly, UDP-based DoS and DDoS attacks share comparable packetlevel patterns, while their distinction becomes clearer when broader ﬂow-level characteristics and behavioral context are considered. Consequently, their discrimination beneﬁts from the temporal and statistical information provided by the ﬂow context. This observation is quantitatively supported by Table 8, where most classes maintain F1-scores above 0.98, conﬁrming the eﬀectiveness of contextual information in preserving discriminative patterns. In contrast, Fig. 5b shows that removing ﬂow context leads to a systematic degradation in classiﬁcation performance, with average accuracy decreasing to 97.90%. The confusion between benign and spooﬁng traﬃc becomes more pronounced and bidirectional, indicating that their separation relies heavily on contextual information rather than instantaneous packet features. Reconnaissance-related traﬃc is also more aﬀected in this setting, suggesting that earlystage packet observations are insuﬃcient to fully capture its behavioral patterns. This degradation is quantitatively conﬁrmed in Table 8, where the F1-score of benign traﬃc drops signiﬁcantly from 0.999 (real packets) to 0.951 under forecasted inputs, representing the largest performance decrease among all classes. In comparison, DoS and DDoS categories remain relatively stable, with F1-scores consistently above 0.96, indicating that their high-intensity and structured traﬃc 10188 VOLUME 7, 2026 TABLE 9. Impact of Flow Context on Proactive Detection (Forecasted Packets). TABLE 10. Flow-Level Real-Time Detection Performance. patterns are inherently more distinguishable at the packet level. A direct comparison between Fig. 5a and Fig. 5b highlights the critical role of the ﬂow context in reducing the ambiguity of classiﬁcation. Incorporating ﬂow-level information improves accuracy by 0.34% and signiﬁcantly mitigates confusion in classes with packet-level characteristics that overlap. As shown in Table 9, this improvement is particularly evident for benign traﬃc, whose F1-score increases from 0.939 to 0.951 when ﬂow context is incorporated, as well as for DNS spooﬁng and vulnerability scanning, which exhibit notable gains in recall. These results demonstrate that the ﬂow context eﬀectively compensates for the loss of temporal information in forecasted packets and enhances the robustness of proactive detection. Proactive Detection Lead-Time Analysis: To quantify the temporal advantage of the proposed proactive mechanism, we evaluate the related detection ratio metric Rd (eq.(10)), where we set Nc = 5 in our implementation, corresponding to the ﬁxed input sequence length used during forecasting model training. Across all attack categories, the proposed framework consistently detects malicious activity after observing only a small fraction of the ﬂow. In particular, ﬂooding-based attacks (e.g., DDoS-UDP Flood and DoS-TCP Flood) are typically detected after observing less than 2%–15% of the ﬂow, while more subtle attacks such as reconnaissance and spooﬁng are detected within approximately 20%–45% of the ﬂow. This corresponds to a substantial reduction (up to 55%–98%) in required observation length compared to conventional ﬂow-based analysis, which requires accumulation of suﬃcient ﬂow statistics before reliable classiﬁcation. Finally, we notice in Fig. 5c that ﬂow-based real-time detection achieves a near-perfect classiﬁcation performance, with an overall accuracy of 99.94% and a macro F1-score of 0.9973. The confusion matrix is almost entirely diagonal, conﬁrming that ﬂow-level features capture the intrinsic behavioral signatures of each traﬃc category. Only negligible confusion is observed, primarily between ArpSpooﬁng and benign traﬃc (0.3%) and between DoS-HTTP Flood and DoS-UDP Flood (0.1%). The small residual confusion between ArpSpooﬁng and benign traﬃc can be attributed to the fact that ARP exchanges are also common in normal network operation, resulting in partially overlapping behavioral patterns at the ﬂow level. Likewise, the limited confusion between DoS-HTTP Flood and DoS-UDP Flood reﬂects similarities in high-volume attack behavior despite diﬀerences in the underlying protocols. This near-perfect behavior is further supported by Table 10, where all classes achieve F1-scores above 0.975, with the majority reaching 0.999 or higher, conﬁrming the strong separability achieved in the ﬂow feature space. Computational Eﬃciency Analysis: Beyond predictive performance, the proposed detection modules exhibit strong computational eﬃciency, supporting low-latency operation within Multi-access Edge Computing (MEC)-assisted environments. The ﬂow-based real-time model achieves an average inference latency of 0.046 ms per packet and a throughput of 21,608 packets/s, while maintaining a compact storage footprint of 11.3 MegaBytes (MB). For proactive packet-level detection, the enhanced model incorporating ﬂow context achieves an inference latency of 0.144 ms per packet and a throughput of 6,941 packets/s. Despite the increased feature dimensionality, the model maintains a compact storage footprint of 17.9 MB while preserving submillisecond inference performance. The reported latency and throughput measurements correspond exclusively to the packet-level and ﬂow-level detection modules, including feature extraction, feature processing, and classiﬁcation with the trained LightGBM models. They do VOLUME 7, 2026 10189 DIAF et al.: LIGHTWEIGHT LANGUAGE-MODEL-DRIVEN AGENTIC FRAMEWORK TABLE 11. Attack Mitigation Mapping Based on D3FEND Strategies. not include the execution time associated with SLM-based packet forecasting, DistilBERT validation, inter-agent coordination, or D3FEND knowledge graph retrieval. Therefore, the reported values should be interpreted as the computational cost of the detection components rather than the complete end-to-end multi-agent framework. Extending the analysis to the full framework remains an important direction for future work. C. MITIGATION ANALYSIS In this work, the evaluation of attack-to-countermeasure mappings aims to validate the correctness of the proposed mitigation reasoning process rather than executing or benchmarking live mitigation actions. Speciﬁcally, we assess whether each detected attack category is properly mapped to standardized ATT&CK techniques and subsequently aligned with appropriate D3FEND-based defensive strategies. This evaluation is performed through structured analysis of the generated mappings with respect to established cybersecurity knowledge bases. Hence, the evaluation is knowledge-driven and emphasizes the quality of mitigation recommendations rather than operational performance metrics such as latency or deployment impact. Accordingly, the reported results assess the correctness and relevance of the generated recommendations rather than the eﬀectiveness of their real-world execution. Table 11 summarizes the mapping between detected attack categories, corresponding ATT&CK techniques, and D3FEND-aligned mitigation strategies. The results demonstrate that the proposed “Mitigation Agent” consistently translates detection outputs into structured defense recommendations using standardized cybersecurity knowledge. To illustrate the complete mitigation reasoning process, consider a detected DDoS-HTTP Flood attack. The Mitigation Agent ﬁrst maps this alert to the ATT&CK technique T1498.002 (Network Denial of Service). Using this technique as input, the D3FEND knowledge graph identiﬁes the corresponding defensive strategies, namely Evict, Harden, and Isolate. These strategies are then translated into concrete recommended actions such as rate limiting, deployment of a Web Application Firewall (WAF), and traﬃc ﬁltering. This example demonstrates the complete reasoning chain from detected attack class to ATT&CK technique, D3FEND strategy selection, and actionable mitigation recommendation. The distribution of mitigation strategies across attack categories is illustrated in Fig. 6. It highlights how diﬀerent FIGURE 6. Distribution of D3FEND-aligned mitigation strategies across different attack categories, illustrating how the Mitigation Agent maps detected attacks to defense actions using ATT&CK-to-D3FEND reasoning. classes of attacks activate distinct defensive behaviors within the proposed framework. In particular, volumetric attacks (DDoS and DoS) are predominantly mapped to Evict and Isolate strategies, reﬂecting their eﬀectiveness in mitigating resource exhaustion through connection reset, traﬃc ﬁltering, and rate limiting. Reconnaissance activities are associated with Detect and Harden strategies, emphasizing monitoring and access control. Spooﬁng attacks exhibit a more diverse mitigation proﬁle, combining Harden, Isolate, and Deceive strategies, reﬂecting the need for layered defenses such as ARP inspection, packet ﬁltering, and DNS traﬃc redirection. D. COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS AND KEY INSIGHTS Table 12 compares the proposed framework with recent agentic, traditional, and proactive intrusion detection approaches across multiple criteria, including architecture, model scale, detection granularity, prediction capability, mitigation support, and accuracy. Recent agentic frameworks such as AgenticCyber [58] and the LLM-based multi-agent system for LAE-IoT [59] (>90%) demonstrate strong detection performance with accuracies 96.8% and over 90%, respectively. However, they remain primarily reactive, relying on ﬂow-level or multimodal data without incorporating proactive prediction. Their dependence on LLMs also introduces signiﬁcant computational overhead, limiting their suitability for resourceconstrained IoT environments. The single-agent IDS [57] achieves a lower accuracy of 79.5%, lacking the multiagent coordination and proactive capabilities. Traditional DL 10190 VOLUME 7, 2026 TABLE 12. Comparative Analysis of Agentic, Traditional and Proactive IDS Approaches. approaches evaluated on IoT datasets [31] and ensemble models such as LightGBM [29] provide eﬃcient ﬂowlevel detection of 96.6% and 99.41%, respectively, yet they remain limited to reactive analysis without forecasting or response mechanisms. Moreover, proactive predictionbased works [16], [17] and multi-agent DDoS defense frameworks [46] demonstrate the feasibility of anticipating or early detecting malicious activity with high accuracy (over 98%), but typically operate without fully integrating detection and mitigation within a uniﬁed pipeline or exploiting multi-granularity packet–ﬂow analysis. In contrast, the proposed framework combines a multi-agent architecture with SLM-based forecasting (99.90% validation rate), hybrid packet-ﬂow detection, and knowledge-driven mitigation. It achieves 98.24% accuracy for proactive detection on forecasted packets and 99.94% for real-time ﬂow-based detection on the CICIoT2023 dataset, thus consistently outperforming existing methods. This comparison highlights the fact that existing methods address individual aspects of intrusion detection, i.e., detection and/or prediction and/or response, whereas the proposed framework integrates these components into a coherent and autonomous pipeline. This integration, combined with multigranularity analysis and lightweight modeling, results in a scalable, interpretable, and end-to-end eﬃcient cybersecurity defense system. VI. CONCLUSION This paper introduced a multi-agent cybersecurity framework for 6G-enabled IoT networks that integrates SLM-based packet forecasting, multi-granularity LightGBM detection at both packet and ﬂow levels, and D3FEND-aligned mitigation within a coordinated architecture. Experiments on the CICIoT2023 dataset showed that the framework achieves 98.24% accuracy in proactive intrusion detection on forecasted packets and 99.94% accuracy in real-time ﬂow-based detection. By unifying prediction, detection, and mitigationrecommendation generation into a single pipeline, our framework demonstrated the promise of lightweight agentic architectures for proactive and adaptive IoT cybersecurity. Despite these strengths, the evaluation relied on a single IoT dataset, which, although recent and comprehensive, may not fully represent the heterogeneity of real-world 6G-enabled IoT deployments or the breadth of emerging attack vectors. Moreover, the mitigation module currently depends on a static D3FEND-based knowledge graph, lim- iting adaptability to novel or evolving threats, and produces structured recommendations rather than autonomously exe- cuted responses. These constraints highlight the need for broader validation and more dynamic mitigation capabilities. Consequently, future work will focus on enabling auto- mated mitigation with veriﬁcation mechanisms supported by SLMs, expanding evaluation across multiple IoT datasets to assess 

